Right.  The problem is unlimited puddings from splits,
so the solution is to count splits against the creation
limit for that species.  Once splits have created 120
per pudding type, you'er done.  I want this to happen to
gremlins, too.  Alternately the problem is death drops
from puddings and setting them to none would remove the
abuse.

> > Thereby also penalising beginning or just generally slow players, unless
> > you set the time high enough, which will make farming possible again.

Would need to be high enough for an extinctionist game.

> I assume you mean that each splitted pudding allows for one split less
> than its origin pudding. Yes, that's one straightforward solution.

I mean each split counts as creating one against the
limit.  Since puddings reproduce by splitting not by
eggs, a split counts as a birth anyways, sorta.

> I think every effectively "unlimited ressource" mechanism in Nethack
> needs some restriction effective in practice. Which other "unlimited
> ressources" are in Nethack?

I don't know if gremlins are already limited.  Get one
on a water level and it doesn't *seem* like it but I've
never counted.

Vault guards keep appearing every 30 moves without any
limit.  It's a way to generate unlimited weapons and
armor at the cost of dropping your alignment and such
faster than you can make up for it killing other monsters
so I don't mind that cost trade-off.  But setting vault
guards to not leave a death-drop and only leave their
initial equipment would make this an unused potential
abuse in most cases.

Folks playing extinctionist can come up with some
other cases that don't respect kill-limit extinction.

I've run arrow and dart traps out but not falling rock
traps.  So I know two types of traps that produce items
are limited and the other type I have no idea.  It's
not like rocks are a limited resource that can be
converted to magical armor.

Prayer can give spellbooks.  In theory there's no limit
to the number of books that could be generated.  As a
practical matter an extinctionist would eventually
run out of possible corpses to offer and then eventually
run out of food to wait.  At some point prayer to cure
hunger would start beating prayer to give books.  And
make one tiny mistake in fine tuning luck to get
crowned, and the timeouts go up much to high to make
it practical to get unlimited books.



